.TH "cbor::item_size" "3" "2026-10-31" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH NAME
cbor::item_size \- checks the first item and gives its size in bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
std::expected<std::size_t, error> item_size(std::string_view encoded);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads the first item in \f[CR]encoded\f[R] to its end and gives its size
in bytes.
It checks that the item is well\-formed: each head, each string and each
element.
Bytes after the item are not read and are not an error.
.PP
The call keeps no stack, so it checks no nesting depth: an item deeper
than \f[CR]limits::nesting_depth\f[R] gives its size.
It does not check tag content, UTF\-8 or duplicate keys.
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the bytes end before the item ends, or a
container claims more items than the bytes left.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: a head is not well\-formed, for example
\f[CR]1c\f[R] or the reserved simple value \f[CR]f8 1f\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]indefinite_length\f[R]: a head uses indefinite length.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]string_length_exceeded\f[R],
\f[CR]container_elements_exceeded\f[R]: a limit is set and a string or a
container goes past it.
.SH NOTES
O(i), i the items, bounded by O(n).
Memory O(1).
No allocation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: [1, 2] followed by 7, 4 bytes\f[R]
cbor::item_size(bytes);                    \f[I]// 3\f[R]
cbor::item_size(bytes.substr(0, 2));       \f[I]// too_little_data\f[R]
\f[I]// bytes: [_ 1]\f[R]
cbor::item_size(bytes);                    \f[I]// indefinite_length\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
mbedded\-cbor(3), cbor::lazy(3), cbor::at_path(3), cbor::item(3), cbor::structs(3), cbor::schema(3), cbor::ref(3), cbor::sequence(3), cbor::sequence_reader(3), cbor::diagnostic_notation(3), cbor::error(3)
